(7169860, 1100095, 138630, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Trotterjay:</b></div><hr/><i> Two people thought this deserved a 10.  Cling to that thought. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks.  I really hadn''t even noticed.  I always look at the % first and this one just took the air right out of me LOL</td>', 'Originally posted by Trotterjay: Two people thought this deserved a 10.  Cling to that thought.   Thanks.  I really hadn''t even noticed.  I always look at the % first and this one just took the air right out of me LOL', '2014-02-05 00:41:11.000000', NULL, 0),
(7169950, 1099564, 47974, '<td valign="top"><img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=152252" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Beatabg</a> Thank you, I am still trying to improve my nature photography.  Gets better every time I go out.<br/> <br/><img alt="'' . substr(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', strrpos(''//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21.gif"/> <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=161988" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">trotterjay</a> Thanks for the wonderful comment.  With telephoto lenses (especially 300mm +) it is pretty much impossible to zone focus.  The DOF is pretty small when using a lens like my 400mm, even stopped way down.  I try to always shoot at 8 (which is the sweet spot for this lens and gives more DOF), but will open up if needed.  This photo I actually took at 5.6 and I am starting think I can shoot at this aperture without worry of softness (but the CA is worse then at 8).  Not going to lie, manual focus is tough.  I miss some shots because of it and get a number of blurry photos.  I have gotten to where I have about a 50% keeper rate (maybe a little better) as far as focus goes, lower when you take composition into account but you would have that problem with autofocus.  I pretty much set up on an animal and continually adjust focus as I wait for the action.  I can tell when a bird (the ones I photograph a lot) is about to strike by its body language. When I see the sign I hit the shutter button.<br/> <br/>Here is a link to my Flickr gallery of wildlife photos, all taken with a manual focus lens. <a href="//www.flickr.com/photos/prehensile-pollex/sets/72157633300434936/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Wildlife Photos</a></td>', ' Beatabg Thank you, I am still trying to improve my nature photography.  Gets better every time I go out.  trotterjay Thanks for the wonderful comment.  With telephoto lenses (especially 300mm +) it is pretty much impossible to zone focus.  The DOF is pretty small when using a lens like my 400mm, even stopped way down.  I try to always shoot at 8 (which is the sweet spot for this lens and gives more DOF), but will open up if needed.  This photo I actually took at 5.6 and I am starting think I can shoot at this aperture without worry of softness (but the CA is worse then at 8).  Not going to lie, manual focus is tough.  I miss some shots because of it and get a number of blurry photos.  I have gotten to where I have about a 50% keeper rate (maybe a little better) as far as focus goes, lower when you take composition into account but you would have that problem with autofocus.  I pretty much set up on an animal and continually adjust focus as I wait for the action.  I can tell when a bird (the ones I photograph a lot) is about to strike by its body language. When I see the sign I hit the shutter button. Here is a link to my Flickr gallery of wildlife photos, all taken with a manual focus lens. Wildlife Photos', '2014-02-05 09:27:34.000000', NULL, 0),
(7170150, 1099744, 103142, '<td valign="top">from the posthumous thread: Sometimes a photo hits just right. There''s something so wonderful about this one, and it resonated so perfectly. I find myself wanting to look at it again and again. <br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=811107" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_811107.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_811107.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/100000-104999/103142/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_811107.jpg"/></a> for <a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1099744" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1867/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1099744.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1867/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1099744.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_challenge/1000-1999/1867/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1099744.jpg"/></a></td>', 'from the posthumous thread: Sometimes a photo hits just right. There''s something so wonderful about this one, and it resonated so perfectly. I find myself wanting to look at it again and again.   for ', '2014-02-05 13:49:41.000000', NULL, 0),
